## Claim Frequency by Fraud Status

| Fraud Reported | Number of Claims |
|---------------|-----------------:|
| No | 753 |
| Yes | 247 |

### Key Observation

The majority of claims (75.3%) were non-fraudulent, while 24.7% were reported as fraudulent.
Although fraudulent claims represent a smaller proportion of the portfolio,
they warrant further investigation due to their potential financial impact on insurers.



#Day 6 Findings


## Claim Frequency by Risk Level

| Risk Level | Number of Policyholders |
|------------|------------------------:|
| Low Risk | 509 |
| Medium Risk | 324 |
| High Risk | 167 |

### Key Observation

More than half of the policyholders (50.9%) were classified as Low Risk, 
while 32.4% and 16.7% were categorized as Medium Risk and High Risk, respectively.
This distribution provides a foundation for developing differentiated pricing strategies based on risk profiles.


## Claim Frequency by Incident Type

| Incident Type            | Number of Claims |
| ------------------------ | ---------------: |
| Multi-vehicle Collision  |              419 |
| Single Vehicle Collision |              403 |
| Vehicle Theft            |               94 |
| Parked Car               |               84 |

### Key Observation

Multi-vehicle collisions were the most frequent type of claim (419), closely followed by single vehicle collisions (403).
Together, these two collision categories account for 822 out of 1,000 claims (82.2%).
Claims involving vehicle theft (94) and parked cars (84) were comparatively uncommon.
This suggests that collision-related incidents are the primary source of insurance claims in the portfolio,
highlighting where insurers may need to focus underwriting, pricing, and loss prevention efforts.
